window.TOPICS = [{"key": "w1", "label": "National Accounts & GDP", "blurb": "GDP measurement, nominal vs real GDP, the GDP deflator and CPI, chain-weighting, the income approach and international (PPP / international-dollar) comparisons."}, {"key": "w2", "label": "Money, Banking & Inflation", "blurb": "The monetary system: monetary base, money multiplier, currency- and reserve-deposit ratios, money velocity, the quantity theory, the classical dichotomy, the Fisher effect and seigniorage."}, {"key": "w3", "label": "Unemployment & the Labour Market", "blurb": "The natural rate of unemployment (job separation & finding rates), frictional vs structural unemployment, efficiency wages, insiders/outsiders and employment protection."}, {"key": "w4", "label": "Circular Flow – Closed Economy", "blurb": "Saving and investment identities, public vs private vs total saving, the paradox of thrift and the real interest rate in a closed economy."}, {"key": "w5", "label": "Circular Flow – Small Open Economy", "blurb": "Net exports, net capital outflow, the trade balance, the real exchange rate, twin deficits and budget-neutral fiscal policy in an open economy."}, {"key": "w6", "label": "The Keynesian Cross", "blurb": "Planned aggregate expenditure, the government-purchases and tax multipliers, automatic stabilizers and short-run output determination with fixed prices."}, {"key": "w7", "label": "The IS-LM Model", "blurb": "The goods market (IS) and money market (LM / liquidity preference), convergence to equilibrium, and central-bank choice between targeting the money supply or the interest rate."}, {"key": "w8", "label": "The Mundell-Fleming Model", "blurb": "The small open economy IS*-LM* model, fiscal and monetary policy under floating vs fixed exchange rates, and world interest-rate shocks."}, {"key": "w9", "label": "AD-AS & the Phillips Curve", "blurb": "Aggregate demand and short-/long-run aggregate supply, the Phillips curve, Okun's law, adaptive vs rational expectations, the Lucas critique and stagflation."}];
window.QUESTIONS = [{"id": "2019-1", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> The growth rate of chain-weighted real GDP does not depend on the base period where the level of chain-weighted real GDP is set equal to nominal GDP.<br><b>II.</b> To compute the growth rate of chain-weighted real GDP between period t and period t+1, we only need to know the quantities of the produced goods and services in period t and t+1, but not their prices.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Chain-weighting computes the growth rate of real GDP as the (geometric) average of the growth rates using base-year t prices and base-year t+1 prices. The <i>level</i> of chain-weighted real GDP is then pinned down by choosing a reference year in which it equals nominal GDP.<br><br><b>I – true.</b> The reference period only rescales the <i>level</i> of the chain-weighted series; it does not change the computed <i>growth rates</i>. So the growth rate is independent of the base period.<br><b>II – not true.</b> The growth rate is a value-weighted average of quantity changes, and the weights are the <i>prices</i>. You therefore need both quantities <i>and</i> prices in t and t+1. Without prices you cannot weight the two goods.<br><br>So I is true and II is not true → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-1", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> The international dollar (I$) is traded on foreign exchange markets outside the U.S.<br><b>II.</b> The fact that GDP per capita in I$ in the U.S. is much higher than in a poor country such as Bangladesh, is partly due to the fact that a lot of goods and services are much more expensive in the U.S. than in Bangladesh.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The international dollar (I$) is a <i>fictitious</i> accounting currency used to compare GDP across countries at purchasing-power parity (PPP). It gives every good the same price everywhere, stripping out price-level differences.<br><br><b>I – not true.</b> The I$ is not a real currency and is not traded on any foreign-exchange market.<br><b>II – not true.</b> Because PPP (I$) comparisons hold prices equal across countries, the higher U.S. GDP per capita in I$ reflects <i>higher quantities</i> of goods and services produced, <b>not</b> higher prices. (Converting at market exchange rates would partly reflect prices, but the I$ precisely removes that.)<br><br>Both statements are not true → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-1", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Only production for which market prices exist is taken into account in the computation of GDP; production for which market prices do not exist is <i>not</i> taken into account.<br><b>II.</b> Production for which market prices exist but which is not sold (and which is therefore added to the inventories of firms) is <i>not</i> taken into account in the computation of GDP.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> GDP measures the market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period.<br><br><b>I – not true.</b> Some production without market prices is still imputed into GDP — e.g. government services (valued at cost) and owner-occupied housing. So it is not true that only market-priced production counts.<br><b>II – not true.</b> Unsold output is added to <i>inventories</i>, and inventory investment <b>is</b> counted in GDP (treated as if the firm bought its own output). So produced-but-unsold goods are included.<br><br>Both statements are not true → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-4", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 4, "topic": "w1", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["The inflation according to the CPI underestimates the change in the cost of living because it ignores substitution possibilities in the consumption basket.", "The inflation according to the CPI overestimates the change in the cost of living because it allows for substitution possibilities in the consumption basket.", "The inflation according to the CPI accurately estimates the change in the cost of living because it allows for substitution possibilities in the consumption basket.", "The inflation according to the CPI overestimates the change in the cost of living because it ignores substitution possibilities in the consumption basket."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The CPI uses a <i>fixed</i> basket of goods (a Laspeyres index). When relative prices change, consumers substitute away from goods that became relatively expensive toward cheaper ones, but the fixed basket does not capture this.<br><br>Because it ignores substitution, the CPI holds quantities fixed at the old, more expensive-to-buy basket, so it <b>overestimates</b> the true rise in the cost of living (substitution bias). It does <i>not</i> allow for substitution.<br><br>→ <b>option d</b>: overestimates because it ignores substitution possibilities.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-1", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "The table below shows the composition of GDP in Japan in 2016 according to the income approach. Are the following statements about Japan in 2016 then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Labour income was 50% of GDP.<br><b>II.</b> Capital income was less than 41.8% of GDP.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (income approach).</b> GDP = taxes less subsidies on production + compensation of employees + operating surplus and mixed income. <i>Labour income</i> = compensation of employees <b>plus the labour part of mixed income</b> (self-employed earn both labour and capital income). <i>Capital income</i> = operating surplus + the capital part of mixed income.<br><br><b>I – not true.</b> Compensation of employees is 50.0%, but labour income also includes part of mixed income, so labour income was <i>more</i> than 50% of GDP.<br><b>II – true.</b> Operating surplus and mixed income together are 41.8%. Capital income is operating surplus plus only <i>part</i> of mixed income, so capital income was <i>less</i> than 41.8% of GDP.<br><br>I not true, II true → <b>option c</b>.", "table": "<table><tr><th>Income approach (% of GDP, 2016)</th><th></th></tr><tr><td>Taxes less subsidies on production and imports</td><td>7.8</td></tr><tr><td>Compensation of employees</td><td>50.0</td></tr><tr><td>Operating surplus and mixed income</td><td>41.8</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-1", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "The table below shows the composition of GDP in Japan in 2016 according to the income approach. Are the following statements about Japan in 2016 then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Capital income was 41.8% of GDP at market prices.<br><b>II.</b> GDP at factor prices was 91.8% of GDP at market prices.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Capital income = operating surplus + the <i>capital part</i> of mixed income (the rest of mixed income is labour income). GDP at factor prices = GDP at market prices − taxes less subsidies on production = compensation of employees + operating surplus and mixed income.<br><br><b>I – not true.</b> Operating surplus + mixed income is 41.8%, but capital income is operating surplus plus only part of mixed income, so capital income was <i>less</i> than 41.8%.<br><b>II – true.</b> GDP at factor prices = 50.0 + 41.8 = 91.8% of GDP at market prices (equivalently, market prices minus the 8.2% of taxes-less-subsidies).<br><br>I not true, II true → <b>option c</b>.", "table": "<table><tr><th>Income approach (% of GDP, 2016)</th><th></th></tr><tr><td>Taxes less subsidies on production and imports</td><td>8.2</td></tr><tr><td>Compensation of employees</td><td>50.0</td></tr><tr><td>Operating surplus and mixed income</td><td>41.8</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-2", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 2, "topic": "w1", "text": "The table below gives nominal and real GDP in The Netherlands in 1986 and 1987. Which of the following statements about this table is correct?", "options": ["From the data in the table follows that inflation according to the CPI in The Netherlands was positive between 1986 and 1987.", "From the data in the table follows that inflation according to the GDP deflator in The Netherlands was positive between 1986 and 1987.", "The data in the table do not imply anything about inflation in The Netherlands between 1986 and 1987.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The GDP deflator = nominal GDP / real GDP (×100). Inflation by the deflator is its growth rate.<br><br>Compute the deflator: 1986 → 220/220 = 1.00; 1987 → 222/225 ≈ 0.987. The deflator <i>fell</i>, so deflator inflation was <b>negative</b>, not positive → option b is wrong.<br>The CPI uses a different basket (and includes import prices), so the table says nothing about CPI inflation → option a is wrong. But the table <i>does</i> tell us about deflator inflation, so option c (\"nothing about inflation\") is also wrong.<br><br>Hence <b>option d</b>: none of the statements is correct.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>1986</th><th>1987</th></tr><tr><td>Nominal GDP (billion €)</td><td>220</td><td>222</td></tr><tr><td>Real GDP (billion €)</td><td>220</td><td>225</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-5", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 5, "topic": "w1", "text": "Which statement about the graph below is correct? The graph shows two inflation series for the U.S. (bold and thin curve).", "options": ["The bold curve is the nominal interest rate in the U.S., and the thin curve is the real interest rate in the U.S.", "The bold curve is the real interest rate in the U.S., and the thin curve is the nominal interest rate in the U.S.", "The bold curve is the inflation according to the GDP deflator in the U.S., and the thin curve is the inflation according to the CPI in the U.S.", "The bold curve is the inflation according to the CPI in the U.S., and the thin curve is the inflation according to the GDP deflator in the U.S."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The CPI includes imported goods (notably oil), while the GDP deflator covers only domestically produced goods. During the 1970s oil crises, import/oil prices spiked, so CPI inflation rose <i>more sharply and more strongly</i> than deflator inflation.<br><br>In the graph the thin curve shows the larger, sharper 1970s peaks — that is the CPI. The bold curve, which moves less in the oil crises, is the GDP deflator. (Both are clearly inflation series, not interest rates: U.S. inflation was positive and the nominal rate normally exceeds the real rate, which doesn't match the picture.)<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: bold = GDP-deflator inflation, thin = CPI inflation.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2021r-5.png"}, {"id": "2026r-1", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 1, "topic": "w2", "text": "Which statement about the graph below is correct? The graph shows two series of money velocity (for currency in circulation) for the U.S. and Japan.", "options": ["The bold curve is money velocity (for currency in circulation) in the U.S., the thin curve is money velocity (for currency in circulation) in Japan.", "The bold curve is money velocity (for currency in circulation) in Japan, the thin curve is money velocity (for currency in circulation) in the U.S.", "The bold curve is the growth rate of money velocity in the U.S., the thin curve is the growth rate of money velocity in Japan.", "The bold curve is the growth rate of money velocity in Japan, the thin curve is the growth rate of money velocity in the U.S."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Money velocity V = P·Y / M. Japan sustained a large monetary expansion for many years, so currency as a share of GDP is much larger in Japan → money velocity is much <b>lower</b> in Japan than in the U.S.<br><br>In the graph the bold curve is the <i>higher</i> series (around 15–23), so it is U.S. velocity; the thin, lower curve is Japan. The curves move fairly smoothly and stay positive, which fits <i>levels</i> of velocity, not growth rates (growth rates would oscillate around zero).<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>: bold = U.S. velocity, thin = Japan velocity.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2026r-1.png"}, {"id": "2026r-2", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 2, "topic": "w2", "text": "Assume an economy where the nominal interest rate is 5%; the real interest rate is 2%; the growth rate of the nominal money supply is 7%; and the growth rate of real GDP is 3%. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["In this economy, both the quantity theory and the classical dichotomy hold.", "In this economy, the quantity theory holds, but the classical dichotomy does not hold.", "In this economy, the quantity theory does not hold, but the classical dichotomy does hold.", "In this economy, neither the quantity theory nor the classical dichotomy holds."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – inflation (Fisher).</b> π = i − r = 5% − 2% = 3%.<br><b>Step 2 – quantity equation in growth rates.</b> ΔM/M + ΔV/V = π + ΔY/Y. So ΔV/V = π + ΔY/Y − ΔM/M = 3% + 3% − 7% = <b>−1%</b>.<br><b>Step 3 – interpret.</b> The quantity theory requires money velocity to be <i>constant</i> (ΔV/V = 0). Here velocity falls by 1%, so the quantity theory does <b>not</b> hold.<br><b>Step 4.</b> The classical dichotomy (nominal variables don't affect real variables) is more general and <i>implies</i> the quantity theory under constant velocity; if the quantity theory fails, the classical dichotomy fails too.<br><br>→ <b>option d</b>: neither holds.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-5", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 5, "topic": "w2", "text": "Which statement is <i>not</i> correct?", "options": ["The quantity equation always holds.", "If the classical dichotomy holds, the quantity theory holds as well.", "If the quantity theory holds, money velocity is constant.", "If the Fisher effect holds, the real interest rate cannot change."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The quantity equation M·V = P·Y is an <i>identity</i> (it defines V), so it always holds — statement 1 is correct. The quantity theory adds the assumption that V is constant, so statement 3 is correct. The classical dichotomy (nominal variables don't affect real ones) implies the quantity theory, so statement 2 is correct.<br><br><b>Statement 4 is NOT correct.</b> The Fisher effect says i = r + π, i.e. a change in expected inflation passes one-for-one into the nominal interest rate. This says nothing that prevents the <i>real</i> interest rate from changing (e.g. a change in saving or investment can move r). So the Fisher effect does not imply a constant real rate.<br><br>The question asks for the incorrect statement → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-9", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 9, "topic": "w2", "text": "Consider an economy described by the liquidity preference model. Assume the economy is initially in equilibrium. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["If the nominal money supply increases, money velocity increases as well.", "If the nominal money supply increases, money velocity remains constant.", "If the nominal money supply increases, money velocity decreases.", "If the nominal money supply increases, then this fact alone does not provide sufficient information to determine whether money velocity increases, remains constant or decreases."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Money velocity V = P·Y / M. In the liquidity-preference (short-run money-market) model, the aggregate price level P and aggregate production/income Y are taken as <i>given/exogenous</i>.<br><br>If the nominal money supply M increases while P and Y are unchanged, then V = P·Y / M must <b>decrease</b>. (Intuitively, more money chasing the same nominal output means each unit of money circulates less.)<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: money velocity decreases.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-5", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 5, "topic": "w2", "text": "Consider an economy which is described by the liquidity preference model. Assume the economy is initially in equilibrium. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["If aggregate production increases, money velocity increases as well.", "If aggregate production increases, money velocity remains constant.", "If aggregate production increases, money velocity decreases.", "If aggregate production increases, then this fact alone does not provide sufficient information to determine whether money velocity increases, remains constant or decreases."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> V = P·Y / M. In the liquidity-preference model the nominal money supply M and the price level P are exogenous.<br><br>If aggregate production Y rises while M and P are held fixed, then V = P·Y / M <b>increases</b>. (More real transactions are supported by the same money stock, so money turns over faster.)<br><br>→ <b>option 1</b>: money velocity increases as well.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-8", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 8, "topic": "w2", "text": "Consider an economy which is described by the liquidity preference model. Assume the economy is initially in equilibrium. If the aggregate price level increases,", "options": ["both the nominal interest rate and money velocity increase.", "the nominal interest rate increases and money velocity decreases.", "the nominal interest rate decreases and money velocity increases.", "both the nominal interest rate and money velocity decrease."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – money market.</b> A higher price level P reduces the <i>real</i> money supply M/P. The real money supply curve shifts left, so to restore money-market equilibrium the <b>nominal interest rate rises</b> (people need a higher rate to be willing to hold less real money).<br><b>Step 2 – velocity.</b> V = P·Y / M. With M and Y exogenous in this model, a higher P raises V directly, so <b>money velocity increases</b>.<br><br>Both increase → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-4", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "The graph below gives M1, the monetary base B and currency in circulation C in the U.S. between 2008 and 2014. Are the following statements about this graph true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> From the information in the graph, we can derive that the money multiplier in the U.S. has declined between 2008 and 2014.<br><b>II.</b> From the information in the graph, we can derive that the reserve-deposit ratio in the U.S. was larger than 1 in 2014.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Money multiplier m = M1 / B, and m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr) where cr = C/D and rr = R/D.<br><br><b>I – true.</b> In 2008 M1 > B; by 2014 M1 < B (the monetary base grew faster due to QE). So M1/B fell below 1 — the money multiplier declined.<br><b>II – true.</b> Since m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr) < 1 in 2014, the denominator exceeds the numerator: cr+rr > cr+1, i.e. <b>rr > 1</b>. Banks held more reserves than deposits (excess reserves during the crisis).<br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2019-4.png"}, {"id": "2020-4", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "The graph gives currency in circulation as a share of nominal GDP (C), the monetary base as a share of nominal GDP (B) and M1 as a share of nominal GDP (M1) in the U.S. from 2014 until 2017. Which of the following can be derived? Note that B has declined and M1 has increased. C has slightly increased (by less than M1).", "options": ["Between 2014 and 2017, both the reserve-deposit ratio and the money multiplier have increased.", "Between 2014 and 2017, the reserve-deposit ratio has increased, but the money multiplier has declined.", "Between 2014 and 2017, the reserve-deposit ratio has declined, but the money multiplier has increased.", "Between 2014 and 2017, both the reserve-deposit ratio and the money multiplier have declined."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – reserves.</b> Reserves R = B − C (monetary base = currency + reserves). As a share of GDP, B fell while C rose slightly, so R/GDP <i>declined</i>. Since M1 = C + D and M1 rose by more than C, deposits D/GDP <i>increased</i>. Therefore the reserve-deposit ratio rr = R/D <b>declined</b>.<br><b>Step 2 – multiplier.</b> m = M1/B. M1 rose and B fell, so M1/B <b>increased</b> — the money multiplier rose. (Equivalently, lower rr raises m.)<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: reserve-deposit ratio declined, money multiplier increased.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2020-4.png"}, {"id": "2021-4", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "The graph gives currency in circulation as a share of nominal GDP (C), the monetary base as a share of nominal GDP (B) and M1 as a share of nominal GDP (M1) in the U.S. from 2008 until 2016. Which of the following can be derived from the graph?", "options": ["Between 2008 and 2016, reserves (held by banks at the Central Bank) as a share of nominal GDP has increased, and the money multiplier (of M1) has increased as well.", "Between 2008 and 2016, reserves as a share of nominal GDP has increased, and the money multiplier (of M1) has decreased.", "Between 2008 and 2016, reserves as a share of nominal GDP has decreased, and the money multiplier (of M1) has increased.", "Between 2008 and 2016, reserves as a share of nominal GDP has decreased, and the money multiplier (of M1) has decreased as well."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – reserves.</b> R = B − C. In the graph the gap between B and C widens strongly after 2008 (QE), so reserves as a share of GDP <b>increased</b>.<br><b>Step 2 – multiplier.</b> m = M1 / B. In 2008 B < M1 (m > 1); by 2016 B > M1 (m < 1). So the money multiplier <b>decreased</b>.<br><br>→ <b>option 2</b>: reserves up, money multiplier down.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2021-4.png"}, {"id": "2021r-4", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "The table below gives currency in circulation C, M1, and the monetary base B in year 1 and year 2. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["From the data in the table follows that both the currency-deposit ratio and the reserve-deposit ratio increased between year 1 and year 2.", "From the data in the table follows that the currency-deposit ratio increased between year 1 and year 2, while the reserve-deposit ratio decreased between year 1 and year 2.", "From the data in the table follows that the currency-deposit ratio decreased between year 1 and year 2, while the reserve-deposit ratio increased between year 1 and year 2.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – deposits.</b> D = M1 − C. Year 1: 100 − 50 = 50. Year 2: 110 − 60 = 50.<br><b>Step 2 – reserves.</b> R = B − C. Year 1: 60 − 50 = 10. Year 2: 80 − 60 = 20.<br><b>Step 3 – ratios.</b> cr = C/D: year 1 = 50/50 = 1; year 2 = 60/50 = 6/5 → <b>increased</b>. rr = R/D: year 1 = 10/50 = 1/5; year 2 = 20/50 = 2/5 → <b>increased</b>.<br><br>Both ratios increased → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>year 1</th><th>year 2</th></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>50</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>M1</td><td>100</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>60</td><td>80</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-4", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "Consider a monetary system which is in equilibrium in period t. Assume now that the reserve-deposit ratio increases, while the currency-deposit ratio and the monetary base remain constant. As a result, the monetary system converges to a new equilibrium in period T. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["In period T, both the money multiplier and currency in circulation are higher than in period t.", "In period T, the money multiplier is lower than in period t, but currency in circulation is higher than in period t.", "In period T, both the money multiplier and currency in circulation are lower than in period t.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr). A higher reserve-deposit ratio rr raises the denominator, so the <b>money multiplier falls</b>. With the monetary base B fixed, M1 = m·B falls too.<br><br><b>Currency.</b> B = C + R is constant and cr = C/D is constant. As banks hold more reserves per deposit, deposits shrink; with cr fixed, C = cr·D shrinks as well. So currency in circulation is <b>lower</b> in period T. (More of the fixed base sits as reserves, less as currency.)<br><br>Both the multiplier and currency are lower → <b>option c</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-4", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 4, "topic": "w2", "text": "Consider a monetary system which is in equilibrium in period t. Assume now that the monetary base increases, while the currency-deposit ratio and the reserve-deposit ratio remain constant. As a result, the monetary system converges to a new equilibrium in period T. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["In period T, both the money multiplier and currency in circulation are higher than in period t.", "In period T, the money multiplier is higher than in period t, but currency in circulation is the same as in period t.", "In period T, the money multiplier is the same as in period t, but currency in circulation is higher than in period t.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr). The multiplier depends <i>only</i> on cr and rr. Since both are unchanged, the <b>money multiplier is the same</b> in period T.<br><br>With m constant, M1 = m·B rises in the same proportion as B. Because cr and rr are fixed, C and R scale up with B too, so <b>currency in circulation is higher</b>.<br><br>→ <b>option c</b>: multiplier unchanged, currency higher.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-3", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 3, "topic": "w2", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["In an economy where a monetary expansion leads to higher inflation and lower real money balances, neither the quantity theory nor the classical dichotomy holds.", "In an economy where a monetary expansion leads to higher inflation and lower real money balances, the quantity theory does not hold, but the classical dichotomy may still hold.", "In an economy where a monetary expansion leads to higher inflation and lower real money balances, the quantity theory may still hold, but the classical dichotomy does not hold.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> From M·V = P·Y we get real money balances M/P = Y/V. The quantity theory assumes V constant and, in the long run, Y exogenous — so M/P would be <i>constant</i>.<br><br>If a monetary expansion causes real money balances M/P to <b>fall</b>, then Y/V fell, so velocity V rose (for given Y): the quantity theory does <b>not</b> hold. And if the quantity theory fails because a nominal change (money growth) affected a real variable, the classical dichotomy fails too.<br><br>Neither holds → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-3", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 3, "topic": "w2", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["If the classical dichotomy holds, money velocity is equal to zero.", "If the classical dichotomy holds, the growth rate of money velocity is equal to zero.", "If the classical dichotomy holds, the growth rate of money velocity is constant (but not necessarily equal to zero).", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The classical dichotomy implies the quantity theory, which requires money velocity V to be <b>constant</b>. A constant V means its growth rate ΔV/V = 0.<br><br>Option a is wrong (V itself is a positive number, not zero). Option c is wrong (a constant growth rate would let V drift; the quantity theory needs V level-constant, i.e. growth rate exactly 0).<br><br>→ <b>option b</b>: the growth rate of money velocity equals zero.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-5", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 5, "topic": "w2", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Seigniorage is the wage of an employee paid in cash.<br><b>II.</b> In a country that experiences hyperinflation, money velocity is usually very high.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>I – not true.</b> Seigniorage is the real revenue a government raises by <i>creating money</i> (the inflation tax), not a wage paid in cash.<br><b>II – true.</b> Under hyperinflation money loses value fast, so people spend it as quickly as possible and hold as little as possible. Money changes hands rapidly → money velocity V = P·Y/M is very high.<br><br>I not true, II true → <b>option c</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-6", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 6, "topic": "w2", "text": "In the 1990s, the US dollar (USD) (in cash) was increasingly used as a store of value by people outside the U.S. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["This increased money velocity of currency in circulation in USD, as well as the money multiplier for M1.", "This increased money velocity of currency in circulation in USD, and decreased the money multiplier for M1.", "This decreased money velocity of currency in circulation in USD, and increased the money multiplier for M1.", "This decreased money velocity of currency in circulation in USD, as well as the money multiplier for M1."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – velocity.</b> V = P·Y / M (for currency). Dollars hoarded abroad do not finance U.S. transactions, so P·Y (U.S. nominal GDP) is unchanged while currency M rises. Hence V = P·Y/M <b>decreases</b>.<br><b>Step 2 – multiplier.</b> m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr), with cr = C/D. More cash held relative to deposits raises the currency-deposit ratio cr, which <b>lowers</b> the money multiplier.<br><br>Both velocity and the multiplier fall → <b>option d</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-9", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 9, "topic": "w2", "text": "In the 1990s, Japan's economy suffered from <i>zombie banks</i>. Which of the following statements about these <i>zombie banks</i> is correct?", "options": ["The central bank failed as <i>lender of last resort</i> to provide sufficient emergency loans to these banks, which then became known as <i>zombie banks</i>.", "<i>Zombie banks</i> were banks that only existed as a legal entity, but not as an operating company.", "The central bank failed to force <i>zombie banks</i> to close or to restructure.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> After Japan's asset bubble burst, many banks were effectively insolvent (their loans would never be repaid) but were kept alive rather than closed or recapitalized. Regulators and the central bank <i>failed to force them to close or restructure</i>. These 'zombie banks' kept lending to equally insolvent 'zombie firms', which choked off credit to healthy firms and prolonged the stagnation.<br><br>Option a misdescribes the lender-of-last-resort role; option b describes a shell company, not a zombie bank.<br><br>→ <b>option c</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-3", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 3, "topic": "w3", "text": "Assume that every period a fraction s of the employed lose their job, while a fraction f of the unemployed find a new job. Assume that the labour force and s and f remain constant over time. s is equal to 5%, f is equal to 95%, and the unemployment rate is 5%. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> The natural unemployment rate is 5%.<br><b>II.</b> The unemployment rate remains constant over time.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – natural rate.</b> In steady state, inflow into unemployment = outflow: s·E = f·U. This gives the natural unemployment rate u* = s/(s+f) = 0.05/(0.05+0.95) = 0.05/1 = <b>5%</b>. So statement I is true.<br><b>Step 2 – dynamics.</b> The actual unemployment rate is already 5% = u*, i.e. the economy is exactly at its natural rate. Since s and f are constant, inflows equal outflows, so the unemployment rate <b>stays constant</b> at 5%. Statement II is true.<br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-6", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 6, "topic": "w3", "text": "Assume that every month, a fraction x of homes that are occupied are made empty and set for sale; and every month, a fraction y of the empty homes are sold, and therefore occupied again. Assume no houses are built or pulled down, so the total number of houses is constant. Assume the housing market is in a long-run equilibrium. The fraction of houses for sale in the total number of houses is then equal to <i>(Hint: recall how we derived the natural unemployment rate!)</i>", "options": ["x/y", "y/x", "x/(x+y)", "y/(x+y)"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (same algebra as the natural unemployment rate).</b> Let A = occupied homes and B = empty-for-sale homes, with total H = A + B constant. In steady state the flow out of occupancy equals the flow back in:<br>x·A = y·B.<br><br>Substitute A = H − B: x(H − B) = y·B → x·H = (x+y)·B → B/H = x/(x+y).<br><br>This is exactly the natural-rate formula u* = s/(s+f), with x playing the role of the separation rate s and y the finding rate f. → <b>option 3</b>: x/(x+y).", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-7", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 7, "topic": "w3", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["The faster technological progress, the lower the natural unemployment rate.", "Faster technological progress does not affect the natural unemployment rate.", "The faster technological progress, the higher the natural unemployment rate.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The natural rate is u* = s/(s+f). Technological progress (creative destruction) can affect <i>both</i> the job-separation rate s (more jobs destroyed) and the job-finding rate f (more jobs created). Because it pushes these in offsetting directions, its net effect on the natural rate is <b>ambiguous / uncertain</b>.<br><br>So none of the definite statements (lower / unaffected / higher) is generally correct → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-7", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 7, "topic": "w3", "text": "In the 1970s, the natural unemployment rate in the Euro area strongly increased, while the natural unemployment rate in the U.S. remained more or less constant. What was according to most economists the most plausible reason for this?", "options": ["Technological progress in the Euro area was slower than in the U.S.", "Because of technological progress, the demand for unskilled labour decreased compared with the demand for skilled labour. As a result, the unemployment rate increased in the Euro area, while in the U.S. the wage of unskilled labour decreased compared with the wage of skilled labour.", "There was much more immigration in the Euro area than in the U.S. As the supply of labour increased, the unemployment rate increased.", "During the 1970s, international trade became much more important for European countries. As European firms faced more foreign competition, labour demand declined, which resulted in a higher unemployment rate."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (skill-biased technological change + wage rigidity).</b> Technological change reduced demand for unskilled relative to skilled labour everywhere. In the <i>flexible</i> U.S. labour market, the relative <b>wage</b> of unskilled workers fell (rising wage inequality) but employment held up. In Europe, rigid wages and generous minimum/union wages prevented unskilled wages from falling, so the adjustment came through higher <b>unemployment</b> instead.<br><br>→ <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-6", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 6, "topic": "w3", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["Stricter employment protection legislation (such as higher severance payments) leads to a lower natural unemployment rate.", "Stricter employment protection legislation (such as higher severance payments) does not affect the natural unemployment rate.", "Stricter employment protection legislation (such as higher severance payments) leads to a higher natural unemployment rate.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> u* = s/(s+f). Stricter employment protection makes firms fire less often → lower job-separation rate s (pushes u* <i>down</i>). But it also makes firms hesitant to hire → lower job-finding rate f (pushes u* <i>up</i>). The two effects work in opposite directions, so the net effect on the natural rate is <b>ambiguous</b>.<br><br>No definite statement holds in general → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-5", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 5, "topic": "w3", "text": "Assume that every month a fraction s of the employed lose their job and become unemployed, while a fraction f of the unemployed find a new job. Assume the labor force and s and f remain constant over time, and that the economy is in a long run equilibrium. Assume that some people who are at working age do not belong to the labor force. What does this imply for the <i>employment rate</i> in this economy? (The question is about the employment rate, not the unemployment rate.)", "options": ["The employment rate is equal to s/(s+f).", "The employment rate is equal to f/(s+f).", "The employment rate is equal to f/s.", "We do not have sufficient information to derive the employment rate."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> From steady state, the natural <i>unemployment</i> rate (as a share of the labour force) is s/(s+f), so the <i>employment</i> share of the labour force is f/(s+f). But the <b>employment rate</b> is usually defined as employed / working-age <i>population</i>, not employed / labour force.<br><br>Since some working-age people are outside the labour force and we are <i>not</i> told how many, we cannot convert the labour-force share into a population share. → <b>option d</b>: insufficient information.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-7", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 7, "topic": "w3", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["The more the interests of the <i>insiders</i> are taken into account in the collective wage bargaining between the trade unions and the employers, the less structural unemployment there will be.", "The more the interests of the <i>outsiders</i> are taken into account in the collective wage bargaining between the trade unions and the employers, the less structural unemployment there will be.", "To what extent the interests of the <i>insiders</i> and the <i>outsiders</i> are taken into account in the collective wage bargaining between the trade unions and the employers does not affect how much structural unemployment there will be.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (insider–outsider).</b> <i>Insiders</i> are employed union members; <i>outsiders</i> are the unemployed. When bargaining weights insiders, unions push for high wages (protecting insiders' pay), which keeps wages above market-clearing and raises structural unemployment. When the interests of <b>outsiders</b> count more, unions accept lower wages so more of the unemployed can be hired → <b>less</b> structural unemployment.<br><br>→ <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-7", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 7, "topic": "w3", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> If the unemployment rate is equal to the natural unemployment rate, there is no structural unemployment and no frictional unemployment.<br><b>II.</b> According to the efficiency wage theory, wages are efficient if the demand for labor is equal to the supply of labor.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>I – not true.</b> The natural rate <i>is</i> the sum of frictional and structural unemployment. Being at the natural rate means there is no <i>cyclical</i> unemployment, but frictional and structural unemployment are still present.<br><b>II – not true.</b> Efficiency-wage theory says firms may find it profitable to pay a wage <i>above</i> the market-clearing level (to raise productivity, reduce turnover, etc.). At that 'efficient' wage, labour demand is <b>less than</b> supply — there is involuntary unemployment. So the statement's definition is wrong.<br><br>Both not true → <b>option d</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-6", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 6, "topic": "w3", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> In general, the unemployment rate is higher in countries with high and long-lasting unemployment benefits.<br><b>II.</b> As a result of the technological progress which the U.S. experienced since the 1970s, the wages of unskilled labor have increased substantially compared with the wages of skilled labor.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> More generous and longer unemployment benefits reduce the urgency to find work (lower the effective job-finding rate f and raise search duration), so the natural/structural unemployment rate tends to be higher.<br><b>II – not true.</b> Skill-biased technological change raised demand for <i>skilled</i> relative to unskilled labour. In the flexible U.S. market this <b>increased</b> the skilled wage relative to the unskilled wage (rising inequality) — the opposite of what the statement says.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-3", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 3, "topic": "w3", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Structural unemployment is caused by real wage rigidities.<br><b>II.</b> If the unemployment rate is equal to the natural unemployment rate, the labour market is in an equilibrium where the demand for labour is equal to the supply of labour.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> Structural unemployment arises because the real wage is stuck <i>above</i> the market-clearing level (minimum wages, union bargaining, efficiency wages) — i.e. real wage rigidity creates a persistent excess supply of labour.<br><b>II – not true.</b> At the natural rate the labour market is 'in equilibrium' only in the sense that unemployment is stable — but it still contains structural unemployment, so labour <b>supply exceeds labour demand</b> at the going wage. Demand = supply would mean <i>no</i> structural unemployment.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-2", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 2, "topic": "w4", "text": "Consider an economy which is described by the circular flow model for a closed economy. An increase in taxes T leads to", "options": ["higher private saving and higher total saving.", "higher private saving and lower total saving.", "lower private saving and higher total saving.", "lower private saving and lower total saving."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – private saving.</b> S_pr = Y − T − C = Y − T − C̄ − c·(Y−T) = (1−c)·(Y−T) + ... . An increase in T of ΔT lowers disposable income, cutting private saving by (1−c)·ΔT. So private saving <b>falls</b>.<br><b>Step 2 – public saving.</b> S_pub = T − G rises by ΔT.<br><b>Step 3 – total saving.</b> S = S_pr + S_pub. The fall in private saving is (1−c)·ΔT; the rise in public saving is ΔT. Net change = ΔT − (1−c)·ΔT = c·ΔT > 0. So total saving <b>rises</b>.<br>(Intuition: households absorb part of the tax by cutting consumption, so not all of the tax comes out of saving.)<br><br>Lower private, higher total → <b>option 3</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-2", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 2, "topic": "w4", "text": "Consider an economy described by the circular flow model for a closed economy. A researcher collects the data below (investment constant at 100 while the interest rate varies 3→6→2). The researcher concludes that investment demand does not depend on the interest rate. This is wrong, because it contradicts the assumption that investment demand decreases as the real interest rate increases. What could have been the source of this mistake?", "options": ["The researcher did not take into account that autonomous consumption did not remain constant.", "The researcher did not take into account that autonomous investment did not remain constant.", "According to the data, total saving remained constant, which is not realistic.", "None of the above could account for the mistake which the researcher made."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The investment function is I = Ī − b·r. Observed investment reflects both the interest rate r <i>and</i> the autonomous component Ī. If Ī shifts over time, the observed (I, r) points trace out shifts of the whole curve, not movements along a fixed curve.<br><br>Here investment stays at 100 even though r changes — consistent with Ī rising when r rises (the autonomous shifts exactly offset the interest effect). So the mistake is ignoring that <b>autonomous investment did not remain constant</b>. (Autonomous consumption does not enter the investment function, ruling out option 1.)<br><br>→ <b>option 2</b>.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>year 1</th><th>year 2</th><th>year 3</th></tr><tr><td>Investment (billion €)</td><td>100</td><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Interest rate (%)</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-7", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 7, "topic": "w4", "text": "Assuming that the economy is initially in equilibrium, are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> According to the circular flow model for a closed economy, an increase in autonomous consumption leads to a new equilibrium with less total saving.<br><b>II.</b> According to the Keynesian Cross model, an increase in autonomous consumption leads to a new equilibrium with less total saving.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true (circular flow / classical).</b> Here Y is exogenous (fixed at its natural level). Total saving S = S_pr + S_pub. A rise in autonomous consumption C̄ lowers private saving (S_pr = (1−c)(Y−T) − C̄) with Y fixed, so <b>total saving falls</b>.<br><b>II – not true (Keynesian Cross / paradox of thrift in reverse).</b> Now Y is endogenous. Higher C̄ raises aggregate expenditure and output Y. In the Keynesian Cross, equilibrium requires S = I; since autonomous investment is unchanged and I doesn't depend on Y here, total saving must return to the <i>same</i> level. The negative effect of higher C̄ on saving and the positive effect of higher Y exactly cancel, so <b>total saving does not change</b>.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-6", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 6, "topic": "w4", "text": "Consider an economy described by the circular flow model for a closed economy, initially in equilibrium. Autonomous consumption increases and autonomous investment declines. The economy converges to a new equilibrium. The government now wants private investment in this new equilibrium to equal private investment in the initial equilibrium. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["The government can achieve this by increasing taxes.", "The government can achieve this by increasing government purchases.", "The government cannot achieve this.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> In a closed economy in goods-market equilibrium, total saving = private investment: S = I. To restore I to its initial level, the government must restore total saving S to its initial level.<br><br>The rise in autonomous consumption C̄ reduced private saving, and hence total saving. To offset this, the government conducts a <i>fiscal contraction</i>: <b>raising taxes</b> increases public saving (S_pub = T − G) and reduces disposable income/consumption, pushing total saving back up to its original level. (The decline in autonomous investment shifts the investment schedule but doesn't change the equilibrium condition S = I — it only affects the equilibrium interest rate.)<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>: increase taxes.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-5", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 5, "topic": "w5", "text": "Consider an economy which is described by the circular flow model for a small open economy. Assume the economy is in equilibrium. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> If net capital outflow is positive, the trade balance is also positive.<br><b>II.</b> If net capital outflow is positive, the real exchange rate appreciates.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> In a small open economy in equilibrium, net capital outflow (NCO) = trade balance (NX): NCO = S − I = NX.<br><br><b>I – true.</b> If NCO > 0, then NX > 0 — the trade balance is positive (the country lends abroad and runs a trade surplus).<br><b>II – not true.</b> A positive NCO corresponds to a trade surplus, which requires a <b>depreciated</b> (more competitive) real exchange rate, not an appreciated one. Appreciation would reduce net exports.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-3", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 3, "topic": "w5", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> In general, the share of exports in GDP is larger in small countries than in large countries.<br><b>II.</b> The U.S. has a trade deficit since the beginning of the 1980s. The reason for this trade deficit is that the U.S. is a large country, such that the share of exports in GDP in the U.S. is smaller than in small countries.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> Small countries are typically specialised and trade a large fraction of their output, so exports are a larger share of GDP than in large, more self-sufficient economies.<br><b>II – not true.</b> The trade balance is exports <i>minus</i> imports. A large country has a small export share, but also a small import share — size alone does not create a deficit. The U.S. trade deficit reflects low national saving relative to investment (S − I = NX < 0), not its size.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-3", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 3, "topic": "w5", "text": "Consider an economy described by the circular flow model for a small open economy. Assume the economy is in a long run equilibrium. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["If the government budget is in deficit, the trade balance is in deficit as well; and vice versa: if the trade balance is in deficit, the government budget is in deficit.", "If the government budget is in deficit, the trade balance is in deficit as well; but if the trade balance is in deficit, the government budget is not necessarily in deficit (but may be in surplus).", "If the trade balance is in deficit, the government budget is in deficit as well; but if the government budget is in deficit, the trade balance is not necessarily in deficit (but may be in surplus).", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory (twin deficits).</b> NX = S − I = (S_pub + S_pr) − I = S_pub + (S_pr − I). A government deficit means S_pub < 0, but the trade balance also depends on private saving minus investment.<br><br>A budget deficit does <i>not</i> guarantee a trade deficit (high private saving could offset it), and a trade deficit does not guarantee a budget deficit. There is no strict one-way implication either direction.<br><br>So neither option 1, 2 nor 3 is correct → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-2", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 2, "topic": "w5", "text": "Consider an economy described by the circular flow model for a small open economy. Assume the goods market is in equilibrium. Exports are 10 billion euro, imports are 15 billion euro, and total saving is 20 billion euro. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["Planned private investment is equal to 15 billion euro.", "Planned private investment is equal to 25 billion euro.", "There is not sufficient information to compute planned private investment.", "None of the answers above is correct."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – net exports.</b> NX = exports − imports = 10 − 15 = −5 billion euro.<br><b>Step 2 – equilibrium condition.</b> In goods-market equilibrium, NX = S − I, so I = S − NX = 20 − (−5) = <b>25 billion euro</b>.<br><br>→ <b>option b</b>: planned private investment = 25 billion euro.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-2", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 2, "topic": "w5", "text": "Are the following statements about the circular flow model for a small open economy true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> If there is excess demand in the goods market, the real exchange rate appreciates.<br><b>II.</b> A budget neutral fiscal expansion (where government purchases and taxes both increase by the same amount) does not affect the trade balance.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> Excess demand in the goods market means desired spending exceeds supply; this bids up the real exchange rate (appreciation), which reduces net exports and restores balance.<br><b>II – not true.</b> A budget-neutral (balanced-budget) rise in G and T lowers total saving: public saving is unchanged (T and G rise equally) but private saving falls because higher T cuts disposable income and consumption by less than the tax. Lower S shifts the excess-saving curve, appreciates the real exchange rate and <b>reduces the trade balance</b>. So it does affect the trade balance.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-2", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 2, "topic": "w5", "text": "Assume the economy is described by the circular flow model of a small open economy, and the government wants to improve the trade balance without changing the real exchange rate. The government can achieve this", "options": ["by (simultaneously) increasing both taxes and import tariffs.", "by increasing taxes and (simultaneously) decreasing government purchases.", "by (simultaneously) decreasing both government purchases and import tariffs.", "with none of the policy measures described above."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The trade balance improves if the excess-saving curve shifts right (more national saving) <i>and/or</i> the net-exports curve shifts right. To raise the trade balance <b>without</b> changing the real exchange rate, you must shift <i>both</i> curves right by matching amounts.<br><br>• A fiscal contraction via <b>higher taxes</b> raises national saving (shifts excess-saving right) → tends to depreciate.<br>• <b>Higher import tariffs</b> shift the net-exports curve right (more NX for a given real exchange rate) → tends to appreciate.<br>Doing both together raises the trade balance while the two opposing exchange-rate effects cancel. → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-3", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 3, "topic": "w5", "text": "The data below are for The Netherlands. The real exchange rate is defined such that an increase implies a real appreciation and a decrease a real depreciation. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> The data for the government surplus and the real interest rate are consistent with the circular flow model for a closed economy.<br><b>II.</b> The data for the government surplus, the trade balance and the real exchange rate are consistent with the circular flow model for a small open economy.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Data:</b> government surplus fell (−2.9 → −4.0), real interest rate rose (−1.0 → 4.2), trade balance fell (2.5 → −1.1), real exchange rate appreciated (97.0 → 104.4).<br><br><b>I – true (closed economy).</b> A falling government surplus means lower public (and total) saving. In the closed-economy model, lower saving shifts the saving curve left and raises the <b>real interest rate</b> — consistent with the data.<br><b>II – true (open economy).</b> In the small open economy, lower public saving shifts the excess-saving curve left, causing a real <b>appreciation</b> and a <b>lower (more negative) trade balance</b> — exactly what the data show.<br><br>Both consistent → <b>option a</b>.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>1975</th><th>1980</th></tr><tr><td>Government surplus (% of GDP)</td><td>-2.9</td><td>-4.0</td></tr><tr><td>Trade balance (% of GDP)</td><td>2.5</td><td>-1.1</td></tr><tr><td>Real interest rate (%)</td><td>-1.0</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>Real exchange rate</td><td>97.0</td><td>104.4</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-5", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 5, "topic": "w5", "text": "Assume the real exchange rate between country A and country B remains constant, the quantity theory holds in both countries, and both have the same growth rate of real GDP. Denote the percentage appreciation of A's currency by Δe/e, inflation by π_A and π_B, and nominal money growth by ΔM_A/M_A and ΔM_B/M_B. Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Δe/e = π_B − π_A (approximately).<br><b>II.</b> Δe/e = ΔM_B/M_B − ΔM_A/M_A (approximately).", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – real exchange rate.</b> Δe/e = Δε/ε + π_B − π_A. With the real rate constant (Δε/ε = 0): Δe/e = π_B − π_A. <b>Statement I is true.</b><br><b>Step 2 – quantity theory.</b> In each country π = ΔM/M − ΔY/Y. With equal real GDP growth (ΔY_A/Y_A = ΔY_B/Y_B), substituting gives<br>Δe/e = (ΔM_B/M_B − ΔY_B/Y_B) − (ΔM_A/M_A − ΔY_A/Y_A) = ΔM_B/M_B − ΔM_A/M_A. <b>Statement II is true.</b><br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>. (This is relative purchasing-power parity combined with the quantity theory.)", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-6", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 6, "topic": "w5", "text": "The graph shows data for many countries 1980–2011. The horizontal axis gives each country's average yearly inflation differential with the U.S. (country inflation minus U.S. inflation). What is then on the vertical axis?", "options": ["The average yearly nominal appreciation 1980–2011 of the country's currency w.r.t. the USD (higher = more appreciated).", "The average yearly nominal depreciation 1980–2011 of the country's currency w.r.t. the USD (higher = more depreciated).", "The average yearly real appreciation 1980–2011 of the country's currency w.r.t. the USD (higher = more appreciated).", "The average yearly real depreciation 1980–2011 of the country's currency w.r.t. the USD (higher = more depreciated)."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (relative PPP).</b> Over long horizons, a currency depreciates nominally against the USD at roughly the rate by which its inflation <i>exceeds</i> U.S. inflation: nominal depreciation ≈ inflation differential. So a positive inflation differential maps to nominal <b>depreciation</b>, and the relationship is upward-sloping and close to the 45° line.<br><br>In the graph, high-inflation countries (Mexico, Iceland) sit high on the vertical axis — they depreciated most. So the vertical axis is average yearly <b>nominal depreciation</b>. → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2021-6.png"}, {"id": "2020-1", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 1, "topic": "w1", "text": "Assume that P denotes the GDP deflator, and that inflation according to the GDP deflator is computed as (P_t − P_{t−1})/P_{t−1}. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> P_t is a weighted average of prices of goods and services where the weights of these prices change over time.<br><b>II.</b> The inflation rate according to the GDP deflator is approximately equal to the growth rate of nominal GDP minus the growth rate of real GDP (as long as the absolute values of both growth rates and the inflation rate are not too large).", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> The GDP deflator is a Paasche-type index: it weights prices by <i>current-period quantities</i>, and those quantities change every period, so the weights change over time.<br><b>II – true.</b> The deflator is P = nominal GDP / real GDP. In growth rates, ΔP/P ≈ Δ(nominalGDP)/nominalGDP − Δ(realGDP)/realGDP, a good approximation when the rates are small.<br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-7", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 7, "topic": "w6", "text": "Consider a closed economy described by the Keynesian Cross model (Y=C+I+G, C=C̄+c(Y−T), I=Ī−b·i, G=Ḡ, T=T̄, with b>0, 0&lt;c&lt;1). The government wants a fiscal expansion increasing the budget deficit by X, either by increasing Ḡ by X or decreasing T̄ by X. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["If the government increases Ḡ by X, the economy converges to a new equilibrium with more aggregate production than when the government decreases T̄ by X.", "If the government increases Ḡ by X, the economy converges to a new equilibrium with the same level of aggregate production as when the government decreases T̄ by X.", "If the government increases Ḡ by X, the economy converges to a new equilibrium with less aggregate production than when the government decreases T̄ by X.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory – multipliers.</b> Government-purchases multiplier = 1/(1−c). Tax multiplier = −c/(1−c).<br><br>• Increase Ḡ by X → ΔY = X/(1−c).<br>• Decrease T̄ by X → ΔY = (c/(1−c))·X.<br><br>Since 1/(1−c) > c/(1−c) (because c < 1), the spending increase raises output by <b>more</b>. Intuition: the first euro of government purchases is spent directly on output, whereas a tax cut is partly saved (only the fraction c is spent).<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-7", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 7, "topic": "w6", "text": "Consider the Keynesian Cross model with a proportional tax system, where taxes T depend on aggregate income Y: T = T̄ + t·Y (t > 0). Such a proportional tax system is an automatic stabilizer,", "options": ["because if government purchases increase, taxes automatically increase as well, such that the government budget remains in equilibrium.", "because if the IS-curve shifts to the left, the interest rate declines, which helps the economy to recover.", "because if Y declines, the multiplier declines as well.", "because of none of the reasons above."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> With proportional taxes the multiplier is 1/(1−c(1−t)), which is <i>smaller</i> than the lump-sum multiplier 1/(1−c). A smaller multiplier means shocks to autonomous spending move output <i>less</i> — that is what makes proportional taxes an automatic stabilizer (it dampens fluctuations).<br><br>Checking the options: a is false (taxes rise with Y, not with G, and the budget need not balance); b describes an interest-rate channel irrelevant to why proportional taxes stabilize; c is false — the multiplier is a constant 1/(1−c(1−t)), it does not change as Y changes. The <i>correct</i> reason (smaller multiplier) is not listed.<br><br>→ <b>option 4</b>: none of the reasons above.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-8", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 8, "topic": "w6", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> According to the Keynesian Cross model, a decrease in the interest rate causes higher planned private investment, which increases the capital stock; as the capital stock increases, firms can produce more, which causes excess supply in the goods market.<br><b>II.</b> According to the Keynesian Cross model, an increase in the aggregate price level leads to a decrease in private consumption (for a given level of the nominal interest rate), and therefore also to a decrease in aggregate production.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>I – not true.</b> In the Keynesian Cross, a lower interest rate raises planned investment, which raises planned <i>expenditure</i> (demand) and output through the multiplier — producing excess <i>demand</i>, not excess supply. The mechanism is not about building up the capital stock and producing more; it is a demand-side story.<br><b>II – not true.</b> The Keynesian Cross is a model in <i>real</i> terms; the aggregate price level is not even a variable in it. So a change in P has no role, and the described consumption channel does not exist in this model.<br><br>Both not true → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-6", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 6, "topic": "w6", "text": "Consider a closed economy described by the Keynesian Cross model. The goods market is in equilibrium when suddenly autonomous investment increases. The economy evolves to a new equilibrium,", "options": ["with higher total saving than in the initial equilibrium.", "with the same total saving as in the initial equilibrium.", "with less total saving than in the initial equilibrium.", "but we don't have sufficient information to know whether total saving in the new equilibrium is higher than, equal to, or less than total saving in the initial equilibrium."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> In equilibrium, total saving equals planned investment: S = I. Autonomous investment Ī rises, so planned investment is higher. Since equilibrium requires S = I, total saving must rise to match.<br><br>Mechanism: higher Ī raises aggregate expenditure and output Y through the multiplier; higher income raises saving until S again equals the (now higher) investment. So total saving is <b>higher</b> in the new equilibrium.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-8", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 8, "topic": "w6", "text": "Consider the Keynesian Cross model (Y=C+I+G, C=C̄+c(Y−T), I=Ī−b·i, G=Ḡ, T=T̄). The goods market is in equilibrium when autonomous consumption changes by ΔC̄. The changes in total saving and aggregate production in the new equilibrium are ΔS and ΔY. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["ΔS = −ΔC̄", "ΔS = (1−c)·ΔY − ΔC̄", "ΔS = c·ΔY − ΔC̄", "None of the answers above is correct."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Derivation.</b> Total saving S = Y − C − G = Y − [C̄ + c(Y−T)] − Ḡ. With T̄ and Ḡ constant (ΔT = ΔG = 0), take the change:<br>ΔS = ΔY − ΔC̄ − c·ΔY = (1−c)·ΔY − ΔC̄.<br><br>→ <b>option b</b>. (This is the saving identity differentiated; it is the Keynesian-cross version of the paradox-of-thrift algebra.)", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-8", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 8, "topic": "w7", "text": "Consider an economy which is described by the IS-LM model. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["A shift along the LM-curve to the right implies an upward shift of the real money demand curve in the liquidity preference model.", "A shift along the LM-curve to the right implies a downward shift of the real money demand curve in the liquidity preference model.", "As long as the position of the LM-curve does not change, the position of the real money demand curve in the liquidity preference model does not change either.", "If aggregate production does not change, a shift of the LM-curve implies a shift of the real money supply curve in the liquidity preference model."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Each point on the LM-curve is a money-market equilibrium. Moving <i>along</i> the LM-curve to the right means higher aggregate production Y (at a higher interest rate). In the liquidity-preference diagram, higher Y raises real money demand (more transactions), shifting the real money <b>demand curve upward/right</b>; the higher interest rate then clears the market.<br><br>Option c is wrong: moving along a fixed LM-curve <i>does</i> shift money demand (because Y changes). Option d is wrong: a shift of the LM-curve with Y fixed is generally due to a change in the money <i>supply</i> or in autonomous money demand; it need not be a money-supply shift specifically.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-11", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 11, "topic": "w7", "text": "Consider an economy described by the IS-LM model. Assume there is excess supply in the goods market and excess supply in the money market. How will the economy then converge to a new equilibrium?", "options": ["First, the interest rate will decline to equilibrate the money market. Once the money market is again in equilibrium, aggregate production and the interest rate will adjust as the economy moves to a new equilibrium in the goods market.", "First, the interest rate will increase to equilibrate the money market. Once the money market is again in equilibrium, aggregate production and the interest rate will adjust as the economy moves to a new equilibrium in the goods market.", "First, aggregate production will decline to equilibrate the goods market. Once the goods market is in equilibrium, aggregate production and the interest rate will adjust as the economy moves to a new equilibrium in the money market.", "First, aggregate production will increase to equilibrate the goods market. Once the goods market is in equilibrium, aggregate production and the interest rate will adjust as the economy moves to a new equilibrium in the money market."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory (adjustment dynamics).</b> The money market clears <i>fast</i> (via the interest rate), while the goods market clears <i>slowly</i> (via production). Excess supply in the money market means M/P &gt; real money demand, so the interest rate must <b>fall</b> to restore money-market equilibrium quickly.<br><br>After the money market clears, the slower goods-market adjustment takes over: with excess supply of goods, production falls, and Y and i adjust along the way to the new IS-LM intersection.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>: interest rate declines first, then output adjusts.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-10", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 10, "topic": "w7", "text": "Consider a closed economy described by the IS-LM model. The central bank tries to stabilize aggregate production as much as possible. Assume the economy is hit only by shocks in the money market (not by shocks in the goods market). Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["The central bank achieves its objective better by keeping the nominal money supply constant than by keeping the interest rate constant; furthermore, if the central bank keeps the nominal money supply constant, it keeps aggregate production perfectly stable.", "The central bank achieves its objective better by keeping the nominal money supply constant than by keeping the interest rate constant; however, even if the central bank keeps the nominal money supply constant, aggregate production is affected by shocks in the money market.", "The central bank achieves its objective better by keeping the interest rate constant than by keeping the nominal money supply constant; furthermore, if the central bank keeps the interest rate constant, it keeps aggregate production perfectly stable.", "The central bank achieves its objective better by keeping the interest rate constant than by keeping the nominal money supply constant; however, even if the central bank keeps the interest rate constant, aggregate production is affected by shocks in the money market."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (Poole's result).</b> If the economy is hit only by <i>money-market</i> (LM) shocks, the central bank should target the <b>interest rate</b>. By fixing the interest rate, it fully absorbs money-market shocks: the LM-curve effectively becomes horizontal, so money-demand/supply disturbances no longer move output. The IS-curve alone then pins down Y.<br><br>With no goods-market shocks and a fixed interest rate, aggregate production is kept <b>perfectly stable</b>. → <b>option 3</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-10", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 10, "topic": "w7", "text": "Consider a closed economy described by the IS-LM model. The Central Bank only observes (and can only control) the nominal money supply and the interest rate, and its ultimate objective is to stabilize aggregate production as much as possible. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["The Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the nominal money supply constant rather than the interest rate constant, both for shocks in planned aggregate expenditures and in autonomous real money demand.", "If the economy is only hit by shocks in planned aggregate expenditures, the Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the nominal money supply constant. However, if the economy is only hit by shocks in autonomous real money demand, the Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the interest rate constant.", "If the economy is only hit by shocks in planned aggregate expenditures, the Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the interest rate constant. However, if the economy is only hit by shocks in autonomous real money demand, the Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the nominal money supply constant.", "The Central Bank reaches its objective better by keeping the interest rate constant rather than the nominal money supply constant, both for shocks in planned aggregate expenditures and in autonomous real money demand."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (Poole).</b> The best instrument depends on the source of shocks:<br>• <b>Goods-market (IS) shocks</b> — shocks to planned aggregate expenditure: keep the <b>money supply</b> constant. An upward-sloping LM lets the interest rate move to partly offset the IS shock, so output moves less.<br>• <b>Money-market (LM) shocks</b> — shocks to autonomous real money demand: keep the <b>interest rate</b> constant. This fully neutralizes the money-demand shock before it reaches output.<br><br>→ <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-9", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 9, "topic": "w7", "text": "The table gives U.S. data for 1992 and 1993: S^pub (public saving, % of GDP) and g_Y (growth rate of real GDP, %). Which statement best describes 1993 (vs 1992)?", "options": ["In 1993, the U.S. government conducted a fiscal expansion. Aggregate production continued to grow at its natural rate, however, as autonomous investment and autonomous consumption slowed down.", "In 1993, the U.S. government conducted a fiscal expansion. As a result, real GDP continued to grow at a faster rate than its natural growth rate.", "In 1993, the U.S. government conducted a fiscal contraction. Aggregate production continued to grow at its natural rate, however, as private investment was stimulated by lower interest rates.", "In 1993, the U.S. government conducted a fiscal contraction. Nevertheless, real GDP continued to grow at a faster rate than its natural growth rate, as both private investment and private consumption were stimulated by lower interest rates."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – fiscal stance.</b> Public saving rose from −5.7% to −4.9% of GDP (the deficit shrank). A smaller deficit means a <b>fiscal contraction</b>. This rules out options 1 and 2.<br><b>Step 2 – growth.</b> Real GDP growth stayed at 3.0%, roughly the natural growth rate. A fiscal contraction would normally slow growth, but here growth was maintained because lower interest rates (the IS-curve shifts left, reducing the interest rate) stimulated private investment, offsetting the contraction.<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: fiscal contraction, growth sustained at the natural rate via lower rates and stronger investment.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>1992</th><th>1993</th></tr><tr><td>S<sup>pub</sup> (% of GDP)</td><td>-5.7</td><td>-4.9</td></tr><tr><td>g<sub>Y</sub> (%)</td><td>3.0</td><td>3.0</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-9", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 9, "topic": "w7", "text": "Which of the following statements about the Greek Depression of 2008-2013 is correct?", "options": ["During the Greek Depression, Greece experienced a fiscal contraction. As a result, the interest rate was lower than in other European countries (such as Germany).", "During the Greek Depression, Greece experienced a fiscal contraction. Nevertheless, the interest rate was higher than in other European countries (such as Germany).", "During the Greek Depression, the Greek government conducted a fiscal expansion. As a result, there was a panic in the market for Greek government bonds, such that the interest rate in Greece became higher than in other European countries (such as Germany).", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Normally a fiscal contraction (smaller deficit) <i>lowers</i> a country's interest rate. But in Greece the panic in the government-bond market dominated: markets discovered the budget deficit and public debt were far larger than believed, so default risk premia soared and the Greek interest rate rose <b>above</b> Germany's — even though Greece was forced into a sharp fiscal contraction (spending cuts and tax hikes).<br><br>So: fiscal contraction, yet a <i>higher</i> interest rate → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-8", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 8, "topic": "w7", "text": "In 1993, President Clinton initiated a deficit reduction program by cutting government spending and increasing taxes in the U.S. How did the American Central Bank support the fiscal authorities in 1993?", "options": ["In 1993, the American Central Bank increased interest rates to lower the inflation rate.", "In 1993, the American Central Bank decreased interest rates to avoid that the government could no longer finance its public debt.", "In 1993, the American Central Bank decreased interest rates to avoid that the economy would slip into a recession.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (policy mix).</b> Clinton's deficit reduction was a fiscal contraction, which by itself would shift the IS-curve left and push the economy toward recession. The Federal Reserve <b>accommodated</b> by lowering interest rates, which stimulated private investment and consumption and kept the economy growing. This is the classic 'tight fiscal / loose monetary' mix.<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: the central bank cut rates to prevent a recession.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-9", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 9, "topic": "w7", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> According to the IS-curve, equilibrium in the goods market requires that aggregate production increases as the interest rate declines. The reason: as the interest rate declines, firms invest more; they acquire a larger capital stock and can produce more, which leads to an increase in aggregate production.<br><b>II.</b> According to the LM-curve, equilibrium in the money market requires that the interest rate increases as aggregate production increases. The reason: as the interest rate increases, total saving increases; firms then invest more and acquire a larger capital stock, so they can produce more.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>I – not true.</b> The IS-curve <i>result</i> (Y rises as i falls) is correct, but the stated <i>reason</i> is wrong. The IS mechanism is about <b>demand</b>: a lower interest rate raises planned investment, which raises planned expenditure and output through the multiplier — not about building up the capital stock to produce more (the IS-LM model does not track capital accumulation).<br><b>II – not true.</b> The LM-curve result (i rises as Y rises) is correct, but the reasoning is wrong. The LM mechanism is about <b>money demand</b>: higher Y raises real money demand; with fixed money supply, the interest rate must rise to clear the money market. It has nothing to do with saving financing investment and capital.<br><br>Both reasons are wrong → <b>option d</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-11", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 11, "topic": "w7", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> During its Lost Decade in the 1990s, Japan's Central Bank systematically increased the nominal money supply, which caused high inflation and a high nominal interest rate.<br><b>II.</b> If the economy is in a liquidity trap, the nominal interest rate is so low that the Central Bank cannot decrease the nominal interest rate anymore to stimulate the economy.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>I – not true.</b> During Japan's Lost Decade there was <b>deflation</b> and a very low nominal interest rate (even a liquidity trap), not high inflation and high rates. The statement has the facts backwards.<br><b>II – true.</b> In a liquidity trap the nominal interest rate is at (or near) its zero lower bound, so conventional monetary policy cannot lower it further to stimulate demand.<br><br>I not true, II true → <b>option c</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-10", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 10, "topic": "w8", "text": "Assume that if the business climate in poor and middle income countries improves, the real interest rate all over the world increases. How would this affect the real exchange rate and the trade balance of the U.S. in the short <i>and</i> in the long run? Assume the circular flow model for a small open economy and the Mundell-Fleming model with floating exchange rates both describe the U.S. well.", "options": ["The real exchange rate of the U.S. will depreciate and net exports will increase, both in the short and in the long run.", "In the short run, the real exchange rate of the U.S. will depreciate and net exports will increase; in the long run, however, this will not be the case.", "In the long run, the real exchange rate of the U.S. will depreciate and net exports will increase; in the short run, however, this will not be the case.", "None of the answers above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Short run (Mundell-Fleming, floating rates).</b> A higher world real interest rate r* raises the domestic real rate; the IS*-curve shifts left, so the exchange rate <b>depreciates</b> and net exports <b>increase</b>.<br><b>Long run (circular flow, small open economy).</b> A higher world real interest rate raises r, which lowers domestic investment; the excess-saving (S−I) curve shifts right, the real exchange rate <b>depreciates</b> and net exports <b>increase</b>.<br><br>The effect is the same in both horizons → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-11", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 11, "topic": "w8", "text": "Consider a small open economy described by the Mundell-Fleming model. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> Fiscal policy is not effective if the economy has floating exchange rates.<br><b>II.</b> The higher the marginal propensity to consume, the more aggregate output increases if government purchases increase and the economy has fixed exchange rates.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> Under <b>floating</b> exchange rates, a fiscal expansion shifts IS* right, which appreciates the currency and crowds out net exports one-for-one. Output is unchanged, so fiscal policy is <i>ineffective</i>.<br><b>II – true.</b> Under <b>fixed</b> exchange rates, fiscal policy <i>is</i> effective. The extent to which higher G raises output is governed by the multiplier 1/(1−c): a higher marginal propensity to consume c means a larger multiplier, so output increases more.<br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-10", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 10, "topic": "w8", "text": "Consider an economy described by the Mundell-Fleming model. Assume floating exchange rates. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["Fiscal policy is effective, as a fiscal expansion leads to an appreciation of the exchange rate.", "Fiscal policy is effective, as a fiscal expansion causes an increase in aggregate production.", "Fiscal policy is not effective, as a fiscal expansion does not affect the exchange rate.", "Fiscal policy is not effective, as a fiscal expansion does not affect aggregate production."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Under floating exchange rates in the Mundell-Fleming model, a fiscal expansion shifts the IS*-curve right. With perfect capital mobility the domestic interest rate is pinned to the world rate, so the adjustment is an <b>appreciation</b> that crowds out net exports completely. Aggregate production is <b>unchanged</b> — fiscal policy is ineffective.<br><br>Note a fiscal expansion <i>does</i> appreciate the currency (so option 3's reasoning is wrong), but output does not change. The correct statement is that fiscal policy is not effective because it does not affect aggregate production → <b>option 4</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-9", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 9, "topic": "w8", "text": "Consider a small open economy described by the Mundell-Fleming model, where net exports depend on both the real exchange rate and aggregate production: NX = NX̄ − d·ε − z·Y (d>0, z>0). Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["A fiscal expansion leads to lower net exports, both in a system with floating exchange rates and in a system with fixed exchange rates.", "A fiscal expansion leads to lower net exports in a system with floating exchange rates, but not in a system with fixed exchange rates.", "A fiscal expansion leads to lower net exports in a system with fixed exchange rates, but not in a system with floating exchange rates.", "A fiscal expansion does not lead to lower net exports, nor in a system with floating exchange rates, nor in a system with fixed exchange rates."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Floating rates.</b> A fiscal expansion shifts IS* right → the currency appreciates (ε rises). Output is unchanged, but the higher real exchange rate reduces NX = NX̄ − d·ε − z·Y. So <b>net exports fall</b>.<br><b>Fixed rates.</b> A fiscal expansion raises aggregate production Y (fiscal policy is effective). Even though ε is fixed, the −z·Y term means higher Y reduces net exports. So <b>net exports fall</b> here too.<br><br>Net exports decline under both regimes → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-10", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 10, "topic": "w8", "text": "Assume that if the business climate in poor and middle income countries improves, the world real interest rate increases. How would this affect the trade balance of the U.S. <i>in the short run</i>? And does the U.S. currently have a trade deficit or a surplus? Assume the Mundell-Fleming model with floating exchange rates describes the U.S. well in the short run.", "options": ["The U.S. currently has a trade surplus. As business confidence abroad increases, the trade surplus is likely to become even larger in the short run.", "The U.S. currently has a trade surplus. As business confidence abroad increases, the trade surplus is likely to decline (and may even turn into a deficit) in the short run.", "The U.S. currently has a trade deficit. As business confidence abroad increases, the trade deficit is likely to become even larger (more negative) in the short run.", "The U.S. currently has a trade deficit. As business confidence abroad increases, the trade deficit is likely to decline (and may even turn into a surplus) in the short run."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Fact.</b> The U.S. has run a <b>trade deficit</b> since the early 1980s (rules out options 1 and 2).<br><b>Mechanism (floating rates).</b> A higher world real interest rate raises the U.S. real rate; the IS*-curve shifts left, the dollar <b>depreciates</b>, and net exports <b>increase</b>. So the trade deficit shrinks (and could even become a surplus).<br><br>→ <b>option d</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-10", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 10, "topic": "w8", "text": "Are the following statements about the Mundell-Fleming model true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> As the real interest rate abroad increases, the domestic real interest rate increases as well and the IS*-curve shifts to the left.<br><b>II.</b> The larger the marginal propensity to consume, the steeper the IS*-curve.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> With perfect capital mobility, the domestic real rate equals the world real rate. When r* rises, r rises; higher r lowers investment and planned expenditure, so the goods-market-clearing output falls for every exchange rate — the IS*-curve shifts <b>left</b>.<br><b>II – not true.</b> A larger marginal propensity to consume raises the multiplier, so a given depreciation raises output by more — the IS*-curve becomes <b>flatter</b>, not steeper.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2026r-12", "exam": "Resit 2026", "year": 2026, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "Consider the AD-AS model with rational expectations. Suppose that the aggregate price level in period t is higher than the aggregate price level which was expected for period t. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["The short-run aggregate supply curve will shift to the left in period t+1.", "The short-run aggregate supply curve will not shift in period t+1.", "The short-run aggregate supply curve will shift to the right in period t+1.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The SRAS curve's position depends on the <i>expected</i> price level for period t+1. Under rational expectations, that depends on what agents expect about the position of the aggregate demand curve (and supply shocks) in period t+1.<br><br>The fact that the price in period t turned out higher than expected tells us nothing by itself about what agents will expect for t+1 — we have no information about the future AD curve. So we cannot say which way SRAS shifts.<br><br>→ <b>option d</b>: none of the statements is correct.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-8", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 8, "topic": "w9", "text": "Between 1998 and 2000, autonomous investment in the U.S. was very high because of the IT revolution. The Federal Reserve kept the nominal interest rate more or less constant. As a result, the inflation rate increased while real GDP growth was above its natural rate. Which statement best describes this period? Assume Okun's Law held.", "options": ["The unemployment rate remained constant at a level below its natural rate, while there was a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve to the left.", "The unemployment rate remained constant at a level below its natural rate, while the economy shifted <i>along</i> the Phillips curve to the left.", "The unemployment rate was declining, while there was a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve to the left.", "The unemployment rate was declining, while the economy shifted <i>along</i> the Phillips curve to the left."], "correct": 3, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – unemployment (Okun's Law).</b> Real GDP grew <i>above</i> its natural rate, so by Okun's Law the unemployment rate was <b>declining</b> (rules out options 1 and 2).<br><b>Step 2 – Phillips curve.</b> Falling unemployment with rising inflation is a <i>movement along</i> a given Phillips curve to the left (down-and-to-the-left along the curve: lower u, higher π). It is not a shift of the curve.<br><br>→ <b>option 4</b>: unemployment declining, economy moving along the Phillips curve to the left.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2019-12", "exam": "Exam 2019", "year": 2019, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["If expectations are completely rational, fully anticipated fiscal or monetary policy affects both the unemployment rate and the inflation rate.", "If expectations are completely rational, fully anticipated fiscal or monetary policy affects the unemployment rate but does not affect the inflation rate.", "If expectations are completely rational, fully anticipated fiscal or monetary policy does not affect the unemployment rate but does affect the inflation rate.", "If expectations are completely rational, fully anticipated fiscal or monetary policy affects neither the unemployment rate nor the inflation rate."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (policy ineffectiveness / rational expectations).</b> If expectations are rational, agents anticipate fully-expected policy and adjust price and inflation expectations immediately. The SRAS curve and Phillips curve shift at once, so <b>real</b> variables (unemployment, aggregate output) are unaffected.<br><br>The policy is absorbed entirely by nominal variables: it <i>does</i> change the aggregate price level and the <b>inflation rate</b>. So anticipated policy affects inflation but not unemployment.<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2020-11", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 11, "topic": "w9", "text": "According to the graph below (inflation vs unemployment in the U.S.), the U.S. economy was dominated from 1981 until 1982 by", "options": ["positive demand shocks.", "negative demand shocks.", "favorable supply shocks.", "unfavorable supply shocks."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Along a given short-run Phillips/AD-AS relation, demand and supply shocks move inflation and unemployment differently:<br>• A <b>negative demand shock</b> lowers inflation <i>and</i> raises unemployment (movement down-and-right along the Phillips curve).<br>• An unfavourable supply shock raises <i>both</i> inflation and unemployment.<br><br>From 1981 to 1982 (the Volcker disinflation), U.S. inflation <b>fell sharply</b> while unemployment <b>rose</b> — the signature of a <b>negative demand shock</b> (the monetary contraction). → <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2020-11.png"}, {"id": "2020-12", "exam": "Exam 2020", "year": 2020, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "Assume the economy is in a long run equilibrium, when suddenly the money supply decreases. Assume this monetary contraction was anticipated by the economic agents, and assume rational expectations. Are the following statements then true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> In the short run, the economy moves along the short run aggregate supply curve to the left.<br><b>II.</b> In the short run, the economy moves along the Phillips curve to the right.<br><b>III.</b> The Phillips curve shifts downwards.", "options": ["In the short run, the economy moves along the short run aggregate supply curve to the left.", "In the short run, the economy moves along the Phillips curve to the right.", "The Phillips curve shifts downwards.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> With rational expectations and a <i>fully anticipated</i> monetary contraction, the SRAS curve and Phillips curve shift immediately so the economy stays at its long-run equilibrium (natural output / natural unemployment). There is <b>no</b> movement <i>along</i> either curve — real variables don't change.<br><br>What happens is that the Phillips curve <b>shifts downwards</b> (lower inflation at the natural unemployment rate), with the economy remaining at the natural rate. So statements about moving along the curves are false; the correct description is the downward shift.<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: the Phillips curve shifts downwards.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-11", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 11, "topic": "w9", "text": "Consider a closed economy described by the IS-LM and the AD-AS model. Assume adaptive expectations, so the expected aggregate price level always equals the price level in the previous period (expected inflation always zero). The economy is in a long run equilibrium when suddenly the nominal money supply increases and then remains constant at this higher level. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["In the long run, the economy moves to a new long run equilibrium with a higher interest rate than before the monetary expansion.", "In the long run, the economy moves to a new long run equilibrium with the same interest rate as before the monetary expansion.", "In the long run, the economy moves to a new long run equilibrium with a lower interest rate than before the monetary expansion.", "In the long run, the economy moves to a new long run equilibrium. But whether the interest rate is higher, equal to, or lower than before depends on the exogenous parameters of the model."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (long-run money neutrality).</b> A one-off rise in the nominal money supply raises the price level proportionally in the long run, leaving the <i>real</i> money supply M/P unchanged. Output returns to its natural level.<br><br>Short run: the LM-curve shifts right and the interest rate falls; but as prices rise, the LM-curve shifts back left to its original position. In the new long-run equilibrium, output is at its natural level and the <b>interest rate is the same</b> as before the monetary expansion (money is neutral).<br><br>→ <b>option 2</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021-12", "exam": "Exam 2021", "year": 2021, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "The Phillips curve is π_t = π_t^e − β·(u_t − u_n), with β>0, and expectations are adaptive so π_t^e = π_{t−1}. The Central Bank wants to decrease the inflation rate while keeping the unemployment rate constant at its natural level. Which of the following statements is then correct?", "options": ["This is possible, both when expectations are adaptive and when expectations are rational.", "This is possible when expectations are adaptive, but this is not possible when expectations are rational.", "This is not possible when expectations are adaptive, but this is possible when expectations are rational.", "This is not possible, nor when expectations are adaptive, nor when expectations are rational."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Adaptive expectations.</b> With π_t^e = π_{t−1}, the Phillips curve becomes π_t − π_{t−1} = −β·(u_t − u_n). To <i>reduce</i> inflation (π_t &lt; π_{t−1}) requires u_t &gt; u_n — unemployment must rise above the natural rate. So disinflation <b>without</b> higher unemployment is <b>not possible</b>.<br><b>Rational expectations.</b> Agents anticipate a credible disinflation, so π_t^e = π_t (no unanticipated shock). The Phillips curve then gives u_t = u_n: inflation can be reduced <b>while keeping unemployment at its natural rate</b>. Possible.<br><br>Not possible under adaptive, possible under rational → <b>option 3</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-10", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 10, "topic": "w9", "text": "Are the following statements about the Short Run Aggregate Supply (SRAS) curve true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> If aggregate output in period t equals the natural level of aggregate output, the aggregate price level in period t equals the price level that was expected for period t in period t−1.<br><b>II.</b> If aggregate output in period t is higher than the natural level, the aggregate price level in period t is higher than the price level that was expected for period t in period t−1.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory (SRAS).</b> The short-run aggregate supply relation is P = P^e + a·(Y − Y_n): the price level exceeds its expected level exactly when output exceeds its natural level.<br><br><b>I – true.</b> If Y = Y_n, then P = P^e: the actual price level equals the expected price level.<br><b>II – true.</b> If Y &gt; Y_n, then P &gt; P^e: the actual price level is higher than expected.<br><br>Both true → <b>option 1</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-11", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 11, "topic": "w9", "text": "Assume rational expectations, and assume the economy is initially in a long run equilibrium. Which statement is then correct?", "options": ["An anticipated demand shock can cause a shift <i>along</i> the Phillips curve, but can also cause a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve.", "An anticipated demand shock causes a shift <i>along</i> the Phillips curve, but does not cause a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve.", "An anticipated demand shock causes a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve, but does not cause a shift <i>along</i> the Phillips curve.", "An anticipated demand shock does not cause a shift <i>along</i> the Phillips curve, nor a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> With rational expectations, an <i>anticipated</i> demand shock is fully built into inflation expectations. The unemployment rate stays at its natural level (no movement <i>along</i> the curve), but expected inflation changes, so the Phillips curve <b>shifts</b>.<br><br>So an anticipated demand shock causes a shift <i>of</i> the Phillips curve but no movement <i>along</i> it. → <b>option 3</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2021r-12", "exam": "Resit 2021", "year": 2021.5, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "The table gives the unemployment rate and inflation in the U.S. in 1981 and 1982. These data are consistent with", "options": ["increasing oil prices and a shift along the Phillips curve to the right.", "increasing oil prices and a shift of the Phillips curve to the right.", "a monetary contraction and a shift along the Phillips curve to the right.", "a monetary contraction and a shift of the Phillips curve to the right."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Data:</b> inflation fell (10.3% → 6.2%) while unemployment rose (7.5% → 9.5%).<br><br><b>Reasoning.</b> <i>Decreasing</i> inflation together with <i>rising</i> unemployment is the signature of a <b>negative demand shock</b> — here the Volcker monetary contraction. On the Phillips diagram this is a movement <b>along</b> the curve to the right (higher u, lower π). An oil-price (supply) shock would have <i>raised</i> inflation, which doesn't match.<br><br>→ <b>option 3</b>: monetary contraction and a shift along the Phillips curve to the right.", "table": "<table><tr><th></th><th>1981</th><th>1982</th></tr><tr><td>Inflation (%)</td><td>10.3</td><td>6.2</td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment rate (%)</td><td>7.5</td><td>9.5</td></tr></table>", "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-8", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 8, "topic": "w9", "text": "The graph below gives Okun's Law in the U.S. What are the variables on the horizontal and vertical axes?", "options": ["The variable on the horizontal axis is the unemployment rate; the variable on the vertical axis is the growth rate of real GDP.", "The variable on the horizontal axis is the change in the unemployment rate; the variable on the vertical axis is the growth rate of real GDP.", "The variable on the horizontal axis is the unemployment rate; the variable on the vertical axis is the change in the growth rate of real GDP.", "The variable on the horizontal axis is the change in the unemployment rate; the variable on the vertical axis is the change in the growth rate of real GDP."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>Theory (Okun's Law).</b> Okun's Law links the <b>change in the unemployment rate</b> to the <b>growth rate of real GDP</b>: when GDP grows faster than its natural rate, unemployment falls (Δu &lt; 0). The relationship is negatively sloped.<br><br>So the horizontal axis is the <i>change</i> in the unemployment rate and the vertical axis is the growth rate of real GDP. → <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": "assets/figures/2022-8.png"}, {"id": "2022-11", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 11, "topic": "w9", "text": "In an economy with stagflation, there is a shift", "options": ["of the Phillips curve to the right.", "of the Phillips curve to the left.", "along the Phillips curve to the right.", "along the Phillips curve to the left."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> Stagflation = rising inflation together with rising unemployment (stagnation + inflation). A movement <i>along</i> the Phillips curve trades off inflation against unemployment (they move in opposite directions), so stagflation cannot be a movement along the curve.<br><br>Stagflation is caused by an <b>unfavourable supply shock</b>, which shifts the whole Phillips curve <b>to the right</b> (higher inflation at every unemployment rate). → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2022-12", "exam": "Exam 2022", "year": 2022, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "If expectations are rational, fiscal and monetary authorities", "options": ["can systematically influence the unemployment rate and the level of aggregate production, and they can reduce the inflation rate without causing a higher unemployment rate.", "can systematically influence the unemployment rate and the level of aggregate production, but they cannot reduce the inflation rate without causing a higher unemployment rate.", "cannot systematically influence the unemployment rate and the level of aggregate production, but they can reduce the inflation rate without causing a higher unemployment rate.", "cannot systematically influence the unemployment rate and the level of aggregate production, and they cannot reduce the inflation rate without causing a higher unemployment rate."], "correct": 2, "explanation": "<b>Theory (Lucas critique / policy ineffectiveness).</b> With rational expectations, agents anticipate systematic policy and adjust price and inflation expectations, so authorities <b>cannot systematically influence</b> real variables (unemployment, output).<br><br>However, a <i>credibly announced</i> disinflation is anticipated, so inflation expectations fall without an unanticipated shock — the authorities <b>can reduce inflation without raising unemployment</b> (costless disinflation in principle).<br><br>→ <b>option c</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-9", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 9, "topic": "w9", "text": "In 2000, autonomous investment and autonomous consumption started to decline in the U.S. As a result, the U.S. economy turned into a recession in 2001, with higher unemployment and lower inflation. Which statement is then the best description for this period in the U.S. according to the IS-LM model and the AD-AS model?", "options": ["Both the IS-curve and the AD-curve shifted to the left.", "The IS-curve shifted to the left, while the economy moved <i>along</i> the AD-curve to the left.", "The AD-curve shifted to the left, while the economy shifted <i>along</i> the IS-curve to the left.", "None of the statements above is correct."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> A fall in autonomous investment and consumption is a negative demand shock. In the IS-LM model it shifts the <b>IS-curve left</b> (less planned expenditure at every interest rate). Aggregate demand — the output at which both goods and money markets clear for each price level — therefore falls, shifting the <b>AD-curve left</b> as well.<br><br>The result is lower output (higher unemployment) and a lower price level (lower inflation), matching the 2001 recession. → <b>option a</b>: both IS and AD shift left.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}, {"id": "2023-12", "exam": "Exam 2023", "year": 2023, "q": 12, "topic": "w9", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true? Base your answer on the AD-AS model.<br><b>I.</b> In the long run, the effect on aggregate production and the aggregate price level of a demand shock that was <i>not</i> anticipated is the same with rational expectations as with adaptive expectations.<br><b>II.</b> In the short run, the effect on aggregate production and the aggregate price level of a demand shock that <i>was</i> anticipated is the same with rational expectations as with adaptive expectations.", "options": ["Statement I is true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is not true."], "correct": 1, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> In the <i>long run</i>, output returns to its natural level and the price level fully adjusts, regardless of how expectations are formed. For an <i>unanticipated</i> shock the long-run outcome is the same under rational and adaptive expectations.<br><b>II – not true.</b> For an <i>anticipated</i> demand shock in the <i>short run</i>, expectations matter: under rational expectations the SRAS shifts immediately so output is unchanged, whereas under adaptive expectations prices/output still move along the short-run curve. The short-run effects differ.<br><br>I true, II not true → <b>option b</b>.", "table": null, "figure": null, "img": null}];
window.PREDICTIONS = [{"id": "pred-01", "topic": "w1", "likely": "Appears in some form almost every year (GDP measurement).", "text": "Nominal GDP grows by 6% and the GDP deflator rises by 2% over one year. Over the same year the CPI rises by 3.5%. Which of the following is then correct?", "options": ["Real GDP grew by approximately 4%, and the difference between CPI and deflator inflation is consistent with rising import prices.", "Real GDP grew by approximately 8%, and CPI and deflator inflation must always be equal.", "Real GDP grew by approximately 3%, and the deflator always exceeds the CPI.", "Real GDP shrank by approximately 2%."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1.</b> Growth of real GDP ≈ growth of nominal GDP − deflator inflation = 6% − 2% = <b>4%</b>.<br><b>Step 2.</b> The CPI includes imported goods while the GDP deflator does not. CPI inflation (3.5%) exceeding deflator inflation (2%) is consistent with <i>import prices</i> rising faster than domestic prices. The two indices need not be equal, and neither always exceeds the other.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-02", "topic": "w2", "likely": "A velocity/quantity-theory computation appears on nearly every exam.", "text": "In an economy the nominal interest rate is 6%, the real interest rate is 1%, the growth rate of the nominal money supply is 8%, and real GDP grows by 2%. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["Money velocity falls by 1%; the quantity theory does not hold.", "Money velocity is constant; the quantity theory holds.", "Money velocity grows by 3%; the classical dichotomy holds.", "Money velocity grows by 1%; the quantity theory holds."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – inflation (Fisher).</b> π = i − r = 6% − 1% = 5%.<br><b>Step 2 – quantity equation in growth rates.</b> ΔM/M + ΔV/V = π + ΔY/Y, so ΔV/V = (π + ΔY/Y) − ΔM/M = (5% + 2%) − 8% = <b>−1%</b>.<br><b>Step 3 – interpret.</b> Velocity is not constant (it falls by 1%), so the quantity theory does <b>not</b> hold.<br><br><i>Exam tip:</i> always lay it out as ΔV/V = π + ΔY/Y − ΔM/M and watch the sign. → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-03", "topic": "w2", "likely": "Money multiplier with cr and rr is a perennial.", "text": "A banking system has currency-deposit ratio cr = 0.5 and reserve-deposit ratio rr = 0.1. The central bank then conducts quantitative easing so banks hold more excess reserves, raising rr to 0.25 (cr and the monetary base unchanged). What happens to the money multiplier and the money supply M1?", "options": ["Both the money multiplier and M1 fall.", "Both the money multiplier and M1 rise.", "The money multiplier rises but M1 falls.", "The money multiplier falls but M1 rises."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Formula.</b> m = (cr+1)/(cr+rr).<br><b>Before:</b> m = (0.5+1)/(0.5+0.1) = 1.5/0.6 = 2.5.<br><b>After:</b> m = 1.5/(0.5+0.25) = 1.5/0.75 = 2.0.<br><br>The multiplier falls from 2.5 to 2.0. With the monetary base B fixed, M1 = m·B falls in the same proportion. → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-04", "topic": "w2", "likely": "Classical dichotomy / Fisher conceptual question recurs.", "text": "Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": ["If the classical dichotomy holds, a change in the growth rate of the nominal money supply leaves the real interest rate unchanged.", "If the classical dichotomy holds, a change in the growth rate of the nominal money supply changes the real interest rate one-for-one.", "The Fisher effect implies that the real interest rate is always constant.", "The quantity equation only holds if money velocity is constant."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The classical dichotomy says nominal variables do not affect real variables. Money growth is a nominal change, so it cannot move the <b>real</b> interest rate — it passes into inflation and (via Fisher, i = r + π) into the nominal rate. So option a is correct and b is wrong.<br>Option c is wrong (Fisher allows r to change for real reasons). Option d is wrong — the quantity <i>equation</i> M·V=P·Y is an identity that always holds; it is the quantity <i>theory</i> that assumes constant V.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-05", "topic": "w3", "likely": "Natural-rate algebra s/(s+f) shows up repeatedly.", "text": "Each month 2% of the employed lose their job and 18% of the unemployed find a job. s and f are constant and the labour force is constant. What is the natural unemployment rate, and if the current unemployment rate is 8%, what happens next?", "options": ["Natural rate is 10%; unemployment will rise toward 10%.", "Natural rate is 10%; unemployment will fall toward 10%.", "Natural rate is 11.1%; unemployment is constant.", "Natural rate is 2%; unemployment will fall."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1.</b> u* = s/(s+f) = 0.02/(0.02+0.18) = 0.02/0.20 = <b>10%</b>.<br><b>Step 2.</b> The current rate (8%) is <i>below</i> the natural rate, so inflows into unemployment exceed outflows → unemployment <b>rises toward 10%</b>.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-06", "topic": "w3", "likely": "Frictional/structural & efficiency-wage concepts recur as true/false.", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> At the natural rate of unemployment there is no cyclical unemployment, but there can be frictional and structural unemployment.<br><b>II.</b> Efficiency-wage theory explains why firms may pay a wage above the market-clearing level, creating involuntary unemployment.", "options": ["Both statements are true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Both statements are not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> The natural rate is frictional + structural unemployment; being at it just means zero cyclical unemployment.<br><b>II – true.</b> Efficiency wages (paid to raise productivity / reduce turnover) sit above market-clearing, so labour supply exceeds demand → involuntary unemployment.<br><br>Both true → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-07", "topic": "w4", "likely": "Paradox of thrift: closed economy vs Keynesian Cross is a classic trap.", "text": "Are the following statements true or not true?<br><b>I.</b> In the classical circular-flow model for a closed economy (Y exogenous), an increase in the saving rate raises total saving.<br><b>II.</b> In the Keynesian Cross (Y endogenous, investment autonomous), an increase in the desire to save leaves total saving unchanged in the new equilibrium.", "options": ["Both statements are true.", "Statement I is true; statement II is not true.", "Statement I is not true; statement II is true.", "Both statements are not true."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>I – true.</b> With Y fixed, saving more directly raises total saving (the real interest rate adjusts to keep S = I).<br><b>II – true (paradox of thrift).</b> With autonomous investment and endogenous Y, equilibrium requires S = I. A stronger desire to save lowers consumption and output until saving falls back to the unchanged level of investment — total saving ends up <i>unchanged</i>.<br><br>Both true → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-08", "topic": "w5", "likely": "NX = S − I identity computation appears most years.", "text": "In a small open economy in equilibrium, private saving is 120, public saving is −30, and planned investment is 70 (all in billion euro). What is the trade balance (net exports)?", "options": ["NX = 20 (surplus).", "NX = −20 (deficit).", "NX = 160.", "NX = 70."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Step 1 – total saving.</b> S = S_pr + S_pub = 120 + (−30) = 90.<br><b>Step 2 – identity.</b> NX = S − I = 90 − 70 = <b>20</b>.<br><br>A positive value means a trade surplus (and net capital outflow of 20). → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-09", "topic": "w5", "likely": "Twin-deficits / real-exchange-rate reasoning recurs.", "text": "In a small open economy in long-run equilibrium, the government cuts taxes (a fiscal expansion), with no change in world interest rates. What happens to national saving, the real exchange rate, and the trade balance?", "options": ["Saving falls, the real exchange rate appreciates, and the trade balance worsens.", "Saving rises, the real exchange rate depreciates, and the trade balance improves.", "Saving falls, the real exchange rate depreciates, and the trade balance improves.", "Nothing changes, because the economy is small and open."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Mechanism.</b> A tax cut raises disposable income and consumption, lowering national saving. Lower saving shifts the excess-saving (S − I) curve left. With a fixed world interest rate, the real exchange rate must <b>appreciate</b> to reduce net exports, so the <b>trade balance worsens</b>. This is the 'twin deficits' link: a bigger budget deficit tends to come with a bigger trade deficit.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-10", "topic": "w6", "likely": "G-multiplier vs T-multiplier comparison is a recurring favourite.", "text": "In the Keynesian Cross with marginal propensity to consume c = 0.8, compare a €10bn increase in government purchases with a €10bn tax cut. By how much does equilibrium output rise in each case?", "options": ["+€50bn for the spending increase; +€40bn for the tax cut.", "+€40bn for the spending increase; +€50bn for the tax cut.", "+€50bn for both.", "+€8bn for the spending increase; +€10bn for the tax cut."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Multipliers.</b> Spending multiplier = 1/(1−c) = 1/0.2 = 5. Tax multiplier = −c/(1−c) = −0.8/0.2 = −4.<br><b>Spending +€10bn:</b> ΔY = 5 × 10 = <b>+€50bn</b>.<br><b>Tax cut €10bn (ΔT = −10):</b> ΔY = −4 × (−10) = <b>+€40bn</b>.<br><br>Government purchases raise output by more because the first euro is spent directly, while part of a tax cut is saved. → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-11", "topic": "w7", "likely": "Poole's instrument-choice result is examined almost every year.", "text": "A central bank wants to stabilize output in the IS-LM model. The economy is hit mainly by shocks to planned aggregate expenditure (IS shocks). Which instrument is better, and why?", "options": ["Keep the money supply constant, because the upward-sloping LM lets the interest rate move to partly offset IS shocks.", "Keep the interest rate constant, because this fully neutralizes IS shocks.", "Keep the money supply constant, because this makes the LM curve horizontal.", "It makes no difference which instrument is used."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Poole's result.</b> For <b>goods-market (IS) shocks</b>, targeting the <i>money supply</i> is better. With a fixed money supply the LM-curve slopes up, so an IS shock partly spends itself on a change in the interest rate rather than output — output moves less. (Fixing the interest rate would let the full IS shock hit output.)<br><br>For money-market (LM) shocks the opposite holds: fix the interest rate. → here, IS shocks → <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-12", "topic": "w7", "likely": "Disequilibrium adjustment dynamics (fast money / slow goods market) recurs.", "text": "In the IS-LM model there is excess demand in the goods market and excess demand in the money market. How does the economy adjust?", "options": ["The interest rate rises first to clear the money market, then output rises to clear the goods market.", "The interest rate falls first, then output falls.", "Output rises first, then the interest rate falls.", "Both markets clear simultaneously with no change in the interest rate."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Theory.</b> The money market clears quickly via the interest rate; the goods market clears slowly via production. Excess demand for money (real money demand > M/P) pushes the <b>interest rate up</b> first. Then, with excess demand for goods, <b>output rises</b> as the economy moves to the IS-LM intersection.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-13", "topic": "w8", "likely": "Mundell-Fleming policy effectiveness under floating vs fixed is core.", "text": "In the Mundell-Fleming model with perfect capital mobility, which pairing is correct?", "options": ["Under floating rates fiscal policy is ineffective and monetary policy is effective; under fixed rates fiscal policy is effective and monetary policy is ineffective.", "Under floating rates both policies are effective.", "Under fixed rates both policies are effective.", "Under floating rates fiscal policy is effective and monetary policy is ineffective."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Floating rates.</b> Fiscal expansion → appreciation crowds out net exports → output unchanged (fiscal <i>ineffective</i>). Monetary expansion → depreciation boosts net exports → output rises (monetary <i>effective</i>).<br><b>Fixed rates.</b> Monetary policy must defend the peg, so it cannot move output (monetary <i>ineffective</i>). Fiscal expansion is not crowded out by exchange-rate moves, so output rises (fiscal <i>effective</i>).<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-14", "topic": "w8", "likely": "World-interest-rate shock on a trade-deficit country recurs (2022, 2026-resit).", "text": "The U.S. runs a trade deficit. The world real interest rate rises. According to the Mundell-Fleming model with floating exchange rates, in the short run the U.S. real exchange rate ___ and net exports ___.", "options": ["depreciates; increase.", "appreciates; decrease.", "depreciates; decrease.", "appreciates; increase."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Mechanism.</b> A higher world real interest rate raises the U.S. real rate; the IS*-curve shifts left. The dollar <b>depreciates</b>, which makes U.S. goods more competitive, so <b>net exports increase</b> (the trade deficit shrinks).<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-15", "topic": "w9", "likely": "Adaptive vs rational disinflation is asked in many forms.", "text": "A central bank announces a credible disinflation. Compare the short-run outcome under adaptive and rational expectations.", "options": ["Under adaptive expectations disinflation raises unemployment temporarily; under rational expectations a credible disinflation can lower inflation with no rise in unemployment.", "Under both, disinflation is costless.", "Under both, disinflation requires higher unemployment.", "Under rational expectations disinflation always raises unemployment more than under adaptive expectations."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Adaptive expectations.</b> π^e = π_{−1}, so reducing inflation requires u > u_n for a while — a <i>sacrifice ratio</i> (temporary recession).<br><b>Rational expectations.</b> A <i>credible</i> announced disinflation is anticipated, so π^e falls immediately; the Phillips curve shifts down and inflation falls with unemployment staying at u_n — disinflation can be (near) costless.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-16", "topic": "w9", "likely": "Demand vs supply shock identification on the Phillips diagram recurs.", "text": "Over one year, inflation rises from 3% to 7% and unemployment rises from 5% to 7%. This pattern is best described as", "options": ["an unfavourable supply shock (stagflation): a shift of the Phillips curve to the right.", "a negative demand shock: a movement along the Phillips curve.", "a positive demand shock: a movement along the Phillips curve.", "a favourable supply shock: a shift of the Phillips curve to the left."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Reasoning.</b> Inflation and unemployment rising <i>together</i> cannot be a movement along the Phillips curve (which trades them off). Both rising is the signature of an <b>unfavourable supply shock</b> (e.g. an oil-price spike) — stagflation — which shifts the whole Phillips curve <b>to the right</b>.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}, {"id": "pred-17", "topic": "w9", "likely": "Okun's law linking GDP growth to Δu recurs.", "text": "Okun's Law for an economy is Δu = −0.5·(g − 3), where g is real GDP growth (%) and 3% is the natural growth rate. If real GDP grows by 5% this year, the unemployment rate", "options": ["falls by about 1 percentage point.", "rises by about 1 percentage point.", "is unchanged.", "falls by about 2.5 percentage points."], "correct": 0, "explanation": "<b>Compute.</b> Δu = −0.5·(5 − 3) = −0.5·2 = <b>−1</b>. A negative Δu means unemployment <b>falls by about 1 percentage point</b>, because growth exceeded its natural rate.<br><br>→ <b>option a</b>.", "table": null}];
